    title: 'Pericardial Disease and Tamponade',
    minutes: 17,
    mechanism: 'Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardial layers and may be infectious, post-infarction, postoperative, autoimmune, malignant, uremic, or idiopathic. Effusion becomes hemodynamically important when intrapericardial pressure restricts filling. Constrictive pericarditis creates a rigid shell that limits diastolic expansion and promotes ventricular interdependence.',
    integration: 'Tamponade equalizes diastolic pressures, reduces stroke volume, and triggers tachycardia and vasoconstriction. Severity depends more on accumulation rate and pericardial compliance than volume alone. Positive-pressure ventilation may further reduce venous return. Constriction causes systemic venous congestion despite preserved or near-preserved EF.',
