    mechanism: 'Infective endocarditis forms infected vegetations on endocardial surfaces, usually valves, after microbial entry and adherence to susceptible tissue or prosthetic material. Destruction can cause acute regurgitation, abscess, conduction block, fistula, or prosthetic dehiscence. Emboli can produce stroke, organ infarction, mycotic aneurysm, or metastatic infection.',
    integration: 'Risk rises with prosthetic valves, prior endocarditis, some congenital lesions, injection drug use, indwelling devices, and bacteremia. Rheumatic fever follows an immune response to group A streptococcal infection and may cause chronic valve scarring, especially mitral stenosis. Myocardial and device infections can coexist.',
    clinical: 'Presentation ranges from fever and murmur to shock, embolic stroke, heart failure, or subtle constitutional symptoms. Blood cultures should be obtained correctly before antibiotics when this does not delay emergency treatment. Echocardiography and microbiology are complementary.',
